      "title": "[Open Culture] Mark Twain Predicts the Internet in 1898: Read His Sci-Fi Crime Story, “From The ‘London Times’ in 1904”",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05173307/Samuel_L_Clemens_1909-e1415682187580.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05173307/Samuel_L_Clemens_1909-e1415682187580.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05173307/Samuel_L_Clemens_1909-e1415682187580.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05173307/Samuel_L_Clemens_1909-e1415682187580.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05173307/Samuel_L_Clemens_1909-e1415682187580.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\nMost people know that Mark Twain wrote about Jim and Huckleberry Finn navigating down the Mississippi. Less well known is that he occasionally dabbled in the burgeoning genre of science fiction. His 1898 short story “The Great Dark” is about a ship that sails across a drop of water on a microscope slide. His novel [&#8230;]",
